# Keys an ephemeral session may not override.
#
# ONE GROUP, AND THE TEST IT PASSES IS WRITTEN BELOW. Everything else an
# ephemeral visitor may change, including their wallpaper.
#
# Markus, 2026-09-24: "i strongly feel we shouldn't lock any of the gnome
# settings. that's enforcing policy, something we're generally reluctant to do.
# and these are ephemeral sessions. the user isn't going to hurt anyone but
# themselves, if they misconfigure gnome."
#
# THE TEST A LOCK MUST PASS, from that ruling and the one exception to it:
#
#   1. reaching the state STRANDS the user with no way back, AND
#   2. there is NO LEGITIMATE USE of the setting in this context.
#
# The second clause is what separates a footgun from a policy. A setting with a
# real use that some users would want is theirs to get wrong; a setting with no
# use at all, whose only reachable outcome is an unrecoverable session, is not a
# preference we are overriding. If you are adding a key here, say which of these
# two it passes, beside the key, in this file.

# The screensaver: a locked screen has NO PASSWORD to unlock it with. The
# session has no PAM and the account has no usable password, so a user who locks
# their screen has ended their session and cannot tell that from a hung desktop.
#
# Markus, 2026-09-24, reversing his own general ruling for this one group: "the
# screensaver must be permanently disabled. as much as i usually don't like
# doing so. this is a clear footgun. there is no good reason to ever turn it on."
#
# That is clause 2 doing the work. Nobody wants a lock screen they cannot
# unlock, so nothing is being taken away from anybody -- which is exactly what
# makes this different from the six background keys that used to sit below.
/org/gnome/desktop/screensaver/lock-enabled
/org/gnome/desktop/screensaver/idle-activation-enabled
# The same clause, for locking by hand (Super+L, the menu): see the key's
# comment in ../10-policy.
/org/gnome/desktop/lockdown/disable-lock-screen
/org/gnome/desktop/session/idle-delay

# WHAT USED TO BE HERE AND IS DELIBERATELY GONE: six background keys, locked
# because "the background is the cue that this session keeps nothing".
#
# THE REASON THEY WENT IS THE OWNER'S, AND IT STANDS ON ITS OWN: locking them
# was enforcing policy, this project is reluctant to do that, and an ephemeral
# visitor who misconfigures their own desktop harms nobody but themselves.
# Changing your own wallpaper is a legitimate thing to want, so clause 2 fails
# and the keys do not belong here. THAT ARGUMENT DEPENDS ON NOTHING ELSE.
#
# A SEPARATE NOTE, WHICH IS NOT THE JUSTIFICATION -- and the distinction is the
# point. There is a design to carry "this session keeps nothing" on the client
# page surround instead, which the visitor does not own and cannot remove; see
# private/DESIGN-per-session-background.md. IT IS NOT IMPLEMENTED.
#
# An earlier version of this comment gave that as the reason the locks went. It
# is a better-sounding reason and a WEAKER one, because it makes the decision
# contingent on unbuilt work: somebody reading it later could reinstate the
# locks on the grounds that the surround was never built, reversing a ruling
# that never rested on it. Record the reason that was given, not the better one
# you thought of.
#
# What IS true and worth acting on: until the surround carries it, this product
# has dropped a signal and put nothing in its place. That is a gap to close, not
# a reason to lock a key back.
